      { id: "m1-a1", title: "What is Web application?", url: "/MVC/WhatIsWebApplication_CorporateTrainer", tags: ["Basics", "HTTP", "Architecture"], intro: "Understand what a web application is, why it matters, and how ASP.NET MVC handles request/response in real projects.", blocks: [
        { heading: "A) Hero + Quick Definition", html: `<p>A <b>web application</b> is software hosted on a web server and accessed through a browser over HTTP/HTTPS.</p><p>Unlike a static website, users can <b>interact</b> with a web app using login, forms, search, workflows, and personalized data.</p><div class="alert alert-info mb-0"><b>Analogy:</b> A website is like a digital brochure; a web application is like an online office where users perform tasks.</div>` },
        { heading: "B) Why it matters + C) Core Concept", html: `<ul><li><b>Scalability:</b> One deployment can serve many users.</li><li><b>Maintainability:</b> Updates happen on the server, not on each client machine.</li><li><b>Business value:</b> Supports workflows like booking, payments, approvals, and dashboards.</li></ul><p><b>Concept flow:</b></p><ol><li>Browser sends request.</li><li>Web server routes request to application logic.</li><li>Application accesses database/services.</li><li>Server returns HTML/JSON response.</li><li>Browser renders updated UI.</li></ol>` },
        { heading: "D) ASP.NET MVC 5 Perspective", html: `<p>In <b>ASP.NET MVC 5 (System.Web)</b>, the request lifecycle typically follows:</p><ul><li><b>Controller</b>: Receives request and coordinates processing.</li><li><b>Model</b>: Contains domain/data/business rules.</li><li><b>View</b>: Renders HTML response for the browser.</li></ul><p>This separation of concerns improves testability and enterprise maintainability.</p>` },
        { heading: "E) Step-by-Step Request/Response", html: `<ol><li>User opens <code>/Products/Details/10</code> in browser.</li><li>Route maps URL to <code>ProductsController.Details(int id)</code>.</li><li>Controller fetches data from Model/Repository.</li><li>Controller returns <code>View(model)</code>.</li><li>Razor view generates HTML.</li><li>Browser renders the final page for the user.</li></ol>` },
        { heading: "F) Website vs Web Application", html: `<table class="table table-sm"><thead><tr><th>Website</th><th>Web Application</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mainly informational pages</td><td>Interactive features and workflows</td></tr><tr><td>Minimal user input</td><td>Heavy user input and processing</td></tr><tr><td>Content-focused</td><td>Task-focused (login, booking, dashboard, etc.)</td></tr><tr><td>Mostly read-only</td><td>Create/update/delete and business transactions</td></tr></tbody></table>` },
        { heading: "G) Common Mistakes + H) Practice", html: `<ul><li><b>Mistake:</b> Putting all logic in controller. <b>Fix:</b> Move business rules to service/model layer.</li><li><b>Mistake:</b> Mixing data access with UI rendering. <b>Fix:</b> Keep Model, View, Controller responsibilities separated.</li><li><b>Mistake:</b> Ignoring validation. <b>Fix:</b> Validate both client-side and server-side.</li></ul><p><b>Mini tasks:</b></p><ol><li>Create a controller action that returns a view with model data.</li><li>Add a form and submit data via POST action.</li><li>Display validation errors in the view.</li></ol>` },
        { heading: "I) Interview Notes + Next Step", html: `<p><b>Interview quick answer:</b> A web application is a server-hosted, browser-accessed, interactive software system that processes user requests and returns dynamic responses.</p><p><b>Next:</b> Continue to <i>What is ASP.NET Web Application?</i> to understand framework-level details.</p>` }
      ] },
      { id: "m1-a2", title: "What is ASP.NET Web Application?", tags: ["ASP.NET"], intro: "Learn ASP.NET web app basics.", blocks: [{ heading: "Overview", html: `<p>An ASP.NET web application is a dynamic website or service built using Microsoft's ASP.NET framework.</p>` }] },
